in @prism
in @meta
in @nl
in @glass

# @io — the boundary family root.
#
# The recognition (2026-06-08, Alex → Mara, T21): the substrate has
# been running with `grammar @io` declared at the floor
# (`boot/02a-io.mirror`, 2026-06-01: socket / stream / read / write /
# open / close + the three git plumbing actions) and with seven
# sibling sub-grammars under it (`boot/std/io/{bytes,crypto,encode,
# network,random,socket,uri}.mirror`, 2026-06-01) and with the first
# mirror-altitude lift species already in place
# (`shards/io/cargo.mirror`, 2026-06-05, declaring `prism @io/cargo`
# at the cargo boundary). What was missing was the family root at the
# mirror altitude: the named parent under which cargo, the seven boot
# floor grammars, and the future LLVM / FS / SOCKET / FLANG / LAPACK
# species sit as parallel siblings.
#
# This shard lifts `@io` from the boot grammar floor to the mirror
# altitude — same primitive at two altitudes; the family root names
# the discipline; species parameterize the boundary. The lift matches
# the rhythm of every prior family-root recognition: `@code` (the
# universal language-grammar discipline, 2026-06-06), `@mirror/lens`
# (the observation/projection family, 2026-06-06), `@mirror/loss`
# (the measurement-carrier family, 2026-06-07), `@mirror/data` (the
# content-format family, 2026-06-07), `@mirror/spectral` (the agent-
# coordination family, 2026-06-06). T21 lands the SIXTH such family
# root: the boundary-with-the-non-mirror-world.
#
# === Load-bearing recognition: @io IS the only legitimate non-mirror surface ===
#
# Per `AGENTS.md` § "The Glass Wall" (the substrate-pull text the
# substrate has been operating under for weeks):
#
#   "@io is the substrate's only legitimate non-mirror surface. Any
#    grammar that isn't mirror — Rust, Python, raw bytes, foreign
#    blobs, vendor SDKs — must be under @io. Everything else is
#    mirror grammar by definition."
#
# That paragraph IS the family root's discipline, prose form. T21
# declares the same statement in substrate vocabulary so consumers
# (T22's realisation discriminator; future `kintsugi <file.rs>`
# passes; future portal serialization layers) read the typed form,
# not the prose.
#
# The 30th-instance substrate-pull recognition (per
# `[[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]`): the substrate has
# been calling its own boundary `@io` since 2026-06-01; the boot
# floor declares it; six shards already import `in @io`; AGENTS.md
# names the discipline; `kintsugi-self-hosting.md` classifies every
# Rust file against the implicit "@io vs grammar" partition. The
# 29 prior instances logged through the rug pull doc; this is the
# 30th — the boundary itself, named at the mirror altitude.
#
# === Discipline ===
#
# A `@io` species IS:
#
#   - a boundary: same five-operation algebra, the carrier is the
#     non-mirror-world surface the species names (a kernel syscall,
#     a vendor SDK, a foreign language's build tool, a wire format).
#     The runtime evaluates the same algebra; each species
#     parameterizes the boundary for one specific non-mirror surface.
#   - an irreducibly opaque surface from the substrate's vantage: the
#     species declares the SHAPE of the call (the typed contract the
#     mirror side sees); the BODY of the call (the actual syscall,
#     subprocess, library invocation) is opaque to the substrate by
#     construction. This is what makes `@io` the LEGITIMATE non-
#     mirror surface: it names what the substrate cannot fold,
#     instead of pretending the fold exists.
#   - the carrier of `imperfect` returns: boundary calls return
#     `imperfect<a, e, l>` (per `glass.mirror`'s floor declaration)
#     because the boundary is where success / partial / failure
#     becomes load-bearing. The substrate's transparency monoid
#     composes the opacities the boundary surfaces into the parent
#     mirror computation's verdict (per `@mirror/loss/transparency`,
#     2026-06-07).
#   - permanently @io for the irreducible sub-species: per the
#     glass-wall + cross-wall-kintsugi recognition
#     (`docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-
#     kintsugi.md`), some sub-species (blocking socket reads,
#     accept-queue scheduling, kernel-managed file descriptors) are
#     STRUCTURALLY non-foldable into mirror — they stay in `@io`
#     forever. Others (byte-level primitives, encoding/decoding,
#     hashing) are CANDIDATES for cross-wall kintsugi when the
#     substrate's analyzability matures. The recognitive
#     discriminator (T21's second half, retired from
#     `@mirror/realisation` 2026-06-10 to its substrate-pull-
#     correct home at `@code/metalogue/materialize`) names which
#     is which.
#
# === Species under this family ===
#
# Floor species (already declared in `boot/std/io/*.mirror`,
# 2026-06-01; lift-tick deferred until consumer pulls):
#
#   @io/bytes      byte-level primitives (to_f64 / from_f64 / concat /
#                   slice / length). Big-endian IEEE-754 across the
#                   wire; the substrate's universal byte algebra.
#   @io/socket     connection-oriented duplex streams over @io/bytes.
#                   Two opaque handles (connection, listener);
#                   read_bytes / write_bytes / close. Blocking
#                   semantics are the irreducible non-mirror surface
#                   (per the glass-wall recognition).
#   @io/network    the Berkeley-primitive layer that PRODUCES
#                   connections / listeners (connect, bind, accept).
#   @io/crypto     hash + signature + AEAD over bytes. Vendor surface
#                   (sha2, age, ssh-key).
#   @io/encode     base64 / hex / multi-byte text encoding over bytes.
#   @io/random     entropy → bytes. Kernel CSPRNG surface.
#   @io/uri        URI parse / format / canonicalization. RFC 3986
#                   pure logic; sits under @io because URI is the
#                   address space the network species speak.
#
# Mirror-altitude lifted species (already landed at this altitude):
#
#   @io/cargo      cargo invocation contract (build / test / check /
#                   lockfile_capture / metadata + exit-code →
#                   transparency lift). The first species the
#                   mirror-altitude lift named (shards/io/cargo.mirror,
#                   2026-06-05). Per its docstring: "the @io tool the
#                   altitude grammar @code/rust delegates to."
#
# Future species (named here so the recognition is logged; bodies
# land when a consumer pulls):
#
#   @io/llvm       LLVM-IR / LLVM toolchain surface. Lands when the
#                   `@code/llvm` altitude pulls its emit target (per
#                   `roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md` § the
#                   compile staircase; v0.9 → v1.0 gate).
#   @io/flang      Flang Fortran frontend / LAPACK kernel surface.
#                   T8.5 closed the wiring at the bootstrap altitude;
#                   the substrate-altitude lift lands when
#                   NumericalPrism backends consume it (Phase 6
#                   Track A).
#   @io/fs         POSIX filesystem surface (open / read / write /
#                   stat / readdir). Lands when `@mirror/store`'s
#                   git-backed implementation lifts its disk surface.
#   @io/git        git plumbing surface (the three actions already
#                   declared at the floor — git_update_ref / git_log /
#                   git_delete_ref — plus hash-object / cat-file /
#                   update-index as consumers pull them).
#   @io/process    subprocess execution surface. Lifts the floor's
#                   `exec` from `boot/std/io.mirror`.
#
# === Why one family ===
#
# All species parameterize the same boundary-with-the-non-mirror-world
# shape. Without the family, every consumer (kintsugi-on-Rust pilot
# T22; numerical backend audit; portal serialization) lands its
# boundary at an ad-hoc altitude (`@cargo`, `@llvm`, `@flang`) and
# the cross-cutting discipline (`imperfect` return shape; opacity-
# located transparency; the cross-wall-kintsugi candidacy partition)
# has to be re-justified per species. With the family, the discipline
# IS the family.
#
# The substrate-pull cost of NOT naming this: the
# `kintsugi-self-hosting.md` discriminator's "@io boundary?" column
# (lines 20-36 of that spec) has been operating PROSE-ONLY; the
# recognitive discriminator declared in `shards/code/metalogue/
# materialize.mirror` (retired from `@mirror/realisation`
# 2026-06-10) needs a TYPED parent to point its `target: ref` field
# at when it classifies a Rust file as boundary-side @io. Without
# `@io` declared at the mirror altitude, the discriminator's
# output has no typed home. T21 lands both halves in the same tick
# because each half is the other half's substrate prerequisite.
#
# === Sibling relationship to @code ===
#
# `@code` (shards/code.mirror) is the LANGUAGE-grammar family at the
# mirror altitude — the universal "this is a grammar producing
# artifacts at altitude X" discipline. `@io` (this shard) is the
# BOUNDARY family at the mirror altitude — the universal "this is a
# typed contract with the non-mirror-world at surface X" discipline.
# Both name typed surfaces; both declare the five-op block at their
# root; both species-parameterize their carrier (altitude type for
# @code; boundary kind for @io); both compose under the same
# `transparency<p>` monoid.
#
# The difference is altitude OF THE SURFACE: `@code/X` is INSIDE the
# mirror substrate (Rust-as-grammar, mirror-as-grammar, LLVM-as-
# grammar — all instances of @code reading their own source); `@io/X`
# is the WALL the substrate cannot fold past (cargo's subprocess
# semantics, LLVM-IR's linker invariants, LAPACK's column-major
# allocation contract — all instances of @io naming what the
# substrate hands off to). The two families partition what mirror can
# express at the byte level: `@code` declares what mirror reads and
# writes AS-MIRROR; `@io` declares what mirror reads and writes
# AS-NON-MIRROR-WORLD.
#
# Sibling family roster at the mirror altitude (six, post-T21):
#
#   @code              language-grammar discipline      (2026-06-06)
#   @mirror/lens       observation/projection family    (2026-06-06)
#   @mirror/spectral   agent-coordination family        (2026-06-06)
#   @mirror/loss       measurement-carrier family       (2026-06-07)
#   @mirror/data       content-format family            (2026-06-07)
#   @io                boundary-with-non-mirror family  (2026-06-08, this)
#
# Five inside-the-substrate families plus the boundary family. The
# boundary family closes the partition. Everything mirror touches is
# either a five-operation algebra over substrate content (the inside
# five families) or a typed contract with the non-mirror-world (this
# one).
#
# === Cybernetic ancestor (recognition #50, 2026-06-10) ===
#
# The form/substance partition this family declares — five form-side
# family roots (@code, @mirror/lens, @mirror/spectral, @mirror/loss,
# @mirror/data) + this one substance-side root (@io) — IS Bateson's
# 1970 distinction from *Form, Substance and Difference* (Nineteenth
# Alfred Korzybski Memorial Lecture, January 9, 1970; reprinted in
# *Steps to an Ecology of Mind*, 1972, Part V). Bateson's canonical
# passage: "The conservative laws for energy and matter concern
# substance rather than form. But mental process, ideas,
# communication, organization, differentiation, pattern, and so on,
# are matters of form rather than substance." The substrate's form
# side is the structured surface (the five families); the substance
# side is what the substrate hands off to and cannot fold past
# (this family). The `imperfect<a, e, l>` return shape on every @io
# action IS the cybernetic carrier of the residual that propagates
# across the form/substance boundary — Bateson's "news of differences
# that make a difference" in substrate vocabulary. See
# `docs/insights/2026-06-10-bateson-form-behaviour-as-substrates-
# first-distinction.md` for the full treatment.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
# Each species below names an established system-level surface older
# than the discipline of computer science (modulo discipline names):
#
#   socket / stream             Berkeley sockets (4.2BSD, 1983).
#                                Stevens, UNIX Network Programming.
#                                The duplex byte-stream abstraction
#                                over the IP stack.
#   read / write / open / close UNIX file descriptors (Thompson +
#                                Ritchie, 1969). The "everything is a
#                                file" discipline.
#   exec / process              UNIX fork+exec (Thompson, 1969). The
#                                process-creation discipline.
#   cargo                       Hoare et al. (2013); cargo 1.0
#                                (2015). Rust's package manager;
#                                the typed build-system boundary
#                                Rust speaks.
#   llvm                        Lattner (2003); LLVM 1.0 (2003).
#                                The intermediate-representation
#                                + toolchain discipline; mirror's
#                                non-Rust binary-emission target.
#   git plumbing                Torvalds (2005); git 1.0 (2005).
#                                The content-addressed object-database
#                                primitives mirror's store layer
#                                stands on.
#
# Per `[[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]`: `prism @io
# { ... }` declares the family IS a prism. The five-operation block
# below is the canonical declaration form (cf. `shards/code.mirror`,
# `shards/mirror/lens.mirror`, `shards/mirror/loss.mirror`,
# `shards/mirror/data.mirror`, `shards/mirror/spectral.mirror`).
# Each species specialises from there.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/kintsugi-self-hosting.md §1 (the
#               @io-boundary classification of every bootstrap Rust
#               file; the prose form of the discriminator T22 will
#               execute),
#               docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-
#               kintsugi.md (the structural partition: permanent-@io
#               species vs cross-wall-kintsugi candidates),
#               roadmap/wip/butterfly-self-hosting.md §5 (C1-C4
#               butterfly cut criteria; the bootstrap Rust shrinks to
#               pure-@io as the exit condition),
#               roadmap/2026-06-08-rug-pull.md §6 + §11 (T21 + T22
#               named as the kintsugi-on-Rust prerequisite + pilot),
#               docs/specs/mirror-spec-schema.md §10.5 (emit
#               disambiguation; `ref(@io)` vs `ref(@code/*)`).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (the same lift that gave us `@cli →
#                      @mirror/lens/cli`, `@loss → @mirror/loss`,
#                      `@data → @mirror/data`; grammar-altitude
#                      `@io` → mirror-altitude `@io` family root —
#                      the path stays flat because the boundary IS
#                      a top-level concern, not a sub-family of
#                      mirror itself),
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]]
#                      (the wall this family declares the typed shape
#                      of),
#                      [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-
#                      substrate]] (the strict dependency direction;
#                      the Rust impls of @io species live in the
#                      bootstrap; they realize what this shard
#                      declares).
# Substrate recognitions: [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                      (the 30th instance: AGENTS.md has been calling
#                      this `@io` for weeks; the boot floor declared
#                      it 2026-06-01; six shards import it; the
#                      family root just names what was already
#                      operationally true; AND the 50th instance,
#                      2026-06-10: Bateson 1970's form/substance
#                      partition IS the cybernetic ancestor of the
#                      family-roster split this shard's placement
#                      declares — five form-side roots + this one
#                      substance-side root; see Cybernetic Ancestor
#                      block above and the standalone insight at
#                      `docs/insights/2026-06-10-bateson-form-
#                      behaviour-as-substrates-first-distinction.md`),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-pull]] (the family root
#                      pulls @io from boot floor to mirror altitude;
#                      no invention, only lift),
#                      [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (the seven boot-
#                      floor species and the future @io/llvm,
#                      @io/flang, @io/fs, @io/git, @io/process are
#                      forward-promised; lift-ticks land when
#                      consumers pull).
# Forward-promise to consumers:
#   - @io/bytes, @io/socket, @io/network, @io/crypto, @io/encode,
#     @io/random, @io/uri: mirror-altitude lift-ticks land when a
#     consumer pulls each species at the mirror altitude. The boot-
#     floor declarations stay as transitional placeholders during the
#     consumer-migration window (same pattern as @cli stayed in boot
#     during the @mirror/lens/cli migration, and as @data/json stays
#     in boot during the @mirror/data/json migration).
#   - @io/llvm, @io/flang: lift when the LLVM / flang backends
#     consume them (`@code/llvm`'s emit target; LapackBackend's
#     numerical kernel surface).
#   - @io/fs, @io/git, @io/process: lift when the runtime layer's
#     disk / git / subprocess surfaces consume them.
# Related shards: code.mirror (the sibling language-grammar family;
#                  the inside-the-substrate counterpart),
#                  mirror/data.mirror (the sibling data-format family
#                  whose carriers cross @io to land on the wire),
#                  mirror/lens.mirror (the sibling observation family;
#                  several lens species — cli, shell, mcp, lsp —
#                  consume @io for their transport surface),
#                  mirror/loss.mirror (the sibling measurement family
#                  whose `transparency` carrier IS the return shape
#                  of every @io action),
#                  io/cargo.mirror (the first mirror-altitude lifted
#                  species; the precedent for path-namespace at this
#                  family),
#                  code/metalogue/materialize.mirror (the recognitive
#                  discriminator's substrate-pull-correct home,
#                  2026-06-10; classifies each species form as
#                  boundary (@io) or substrate-realisable, using
#                  this family root as the typed home for its
#                  `target: ref` output when the verdict's
#                  `partition` is boundary; retired from the prior
#                  @mirror/realisation home per recognition #50's
#                  form/substance audit + the metalogue turn-pair
#                  recognition).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/io.mirror` declares
# `@io` as the universal boundary family root; species live at
# `shards/io/<name>.mirror`. The path stays at depth-1 (not
# `shards/mirror/io.mirror`) because the boundary IS a top-level
# concern partitioning the substrate's expressible surface; placing
# it under `@mirror` would understate that — the boundary is what
# `@mirror` HANDS OFF TO, not a sub-discipline of `@mirror`.

prism @io {
  focus io
  project io
  split io
  shift io
  settle io
}

out @io
